{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>But the more they afflicted it, so did it multiply, etc.</b> How sweet is the pleasantness of Scripture according to what is written in the Zohar (vol. II, 95a) on the verse (Ecclesiastes 8:9) ״עֵת אֲשֶׁר שָׁלַט הָאָדָם בְּאָדָם לְרַע לוֹ״ — for through the medium of affliction and suffering, the aspect of good is refined from the evil and cleaves to the portion of good, and the aspect of evil is refined from the portion of good and cleaves to the aspect of evil. These two particulars are alluded to in His saying ״לְרַע לוֹ״. And this itself is what Scripture says: ״וְכַאֲשֶׁר יְעַנּוּ אֹתוֹ כֵן״ — according to the measure of the affliction, they would bring forth and refine the portion of good, and the portion of good would multiply, ״וְכֵן יִפְרֹץ״. Or it may allude in <b>וְכֵן יִפְרֹץ</b> that the portion of evil that had been attached to them would break forth and depart from them — and this is the refining that HaShem refined them in the iron furnace in Egypt." "And according to the plain sense of Scripture, it further intends that when the Egyptians increased their affliction, HaShem would multiply Israel so that they would be helped in the labor, and He would give them strength. And this is His saying <b>כֵּן יִרְבֶּה</b> — according to the measure that the laborers would suffice for the labor, and also HaShem gives them strength according to the measure that diminishes from their strength on account of the affliction. And when the Egyptians saw this, they would enslave them בְּפָרֶךְ — according to its translation, in harshness."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "In the Ecclesiastes quotation, the Hebrew בְּאָדָם (with a dagesh in the bet, as a prefixed preposition) appears in the source text without the dagesh; I have reproduced the text as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:07.320810380Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}